{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-129267",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's reverse the limits of integration on the known result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\int }_{2}^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-⟨10,\\,4,\\,-2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-10,\\,-4,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the vector addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}f(t)+⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{2}⟨2,\\,-1,\\,-2⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}⟨-10,\\,-4,\\,2⟩+⟨2t,\\,-t,\\,-2t⟩{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =⟨-15,\\,-6,\\,3⟩+⟨4,\\,-2,\\,-4⟩\\text{ } \\\\  & =⟨-11,\\,-8,\\,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
